/*!

@page start_guide Start a project

@tableofcontents

From an empty directory to a window on screen, in three files and three
commands.  There is nothing to download by hand and nothing to install
system-wide: CMake fetches Lumina, pins it to a revision you choose, and builds
it alongside your own code.

This page is about the plumbing — getting the dependency in and the build
working.  @ref first_window explains what the C code actually does, line by
line.


@section start_requirements What you need first

- A __C compiler__.  Visual Studio on Windows, Xcode's command line tools on
  macOS, GCC or Clang elsewhere.
- __CMake 3.16 or newer__.  Check with `cmake --version`.
- __Git__, which CMake uses to fetch Lumina.

On Linux you also need the development packages for your display server;
Lumina needs them to build, and they are not installed by default.  On Debian
and derivatives:

@code{.sh}
sudo apt install xorg-dev
@endcode

@ref compile_deps lists what the other distributions and Wayland need.

Windows and macOS need nothing beyond the compiler.


@section start_layout The project

Three files, one of which is a directory:

@code{.txt}
hello-lumina/
├── CMakeLists.txt
└── src/
    └── main.c
@endcode

Create them:

@code{.sh}
mkdir -p hello-lumina/src
cd hello-lumina
@endcode

@subsection start_cmake CMakeLists.txt

@code{.cmake}
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
project(hello-lumina LANGUAGES C)

include(FetchContent)

FetchContent_Declare(
    lumina
    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/Orvaen/Lumina.git
    GIT_TAG        master
    GIT_SHALLOW    TRUE
)
FetchContent_MakeAvailable(lumina)

find_package(OpenGL REQUIRED)

add_executable(hello src/main.c)
target_link_libraries(hello PRIVATE lumina OpenGL::GL)
set_target_properties(hello PROPERTIES C_STANDARD 99)
@endcode

What each part is for:

- `FetchContent_Declare` records __where__ Lumina comes from and __which
  revision__.  Nothing is downloaded yet.
- `FetchContent_MakeAvailable` clones it into the build directory the first
  time you configure, then adds it to your build.  From then on it is cached;
  it does not clone again on every build.
- `GIT_SHALLOW TRUE` fetches one revision instead of the whole history.  Drop
  it if you pin to a commit that is not the tip of a branch.
- `find_package(OpenGL REQUIRED)` is for __your__ code, not for Lumina.  See
  @ref start_opengl below.
- The target is called `lumina`, whichever way you brought it in.
- `C_STANDARD 99` is what the descriptor syntax in the next file needs.

@note `GIT_TAG master` gets you running quickly, but it means a later build can
pick up different code. Read @ref start_pin before you commit this file.

@subsection start_main src/main.c

@code{.c}
#include <Lumina/lumina.h>

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

static void error_callback(int error, const char* description)
{
    fprintf(stderr, "Lumina error: %s\n", description);
}

static void key_callback(LUMwindow* window,
                         int key, int scancode, int action, int mods)
{
    if (key == LUM_KEY_ESCAPE && action == LUM_PRESS)
        lumSetWindowShouldClose(window, LUM_TRUE);
}

int main(void)
{
    LUMwindow* window;

    lumSetErrorCallback(error_callback);

    if (!lumInit())
        return EXIT_FAILURE;

    window = lumCreateWindowEx(&(LUMwindowdesc)
    {
        .width       = 640,
        .height      = 480,
        .title       = "Hello Lumina",
        .makeCurrent = LUM_ON
    });

    if (!window)
    {
        lumTerminate();
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    lumSetKeyCallback(window, key_callback);
    lumSwapInterval(1);

    printf("Lumina %s\n", lumGetVersionString());

    while (!lumWindowShouldClose(window))
    {
        int width, height;
        lumGetFramebufferSize(window, &width, &height);
        glViewport(0, 0, width, height);

        glClearColor(0.18f, 0.12f, 0.30f, 1.f);
        glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT);

        lumSwapBuffers(window);
        lumPollEvents();
    }

    lumDestroyWindow(window);
    lumTerminate();
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
@endcode

@ref first_window takes this program apart one piece at a time.  For now it is
enough that it opens a window, fills it, and closes on _Escape_.


@section start_build Build it and run it

Configure once.  The first run clones Lumina, so it takes a few seconds longer
than the ones after it:

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build
@endcode

Then build:

@code{.sh}
cmake --build build
@endcode

On Visual Studio and Xcode, which hold every configuration in one build
directory, say which one you want:

@code{.sh}
cmake --build build --config Release
@endcode

On single-configuration generators such as Makefiles and Ninja, the choice is
made when you configure instead:

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
@endcode

Run it:

@code{.sh}
./build/hello              # Makefiles, Ninja
./build/Release/hello.exe  # Visual Studio
@endcode

A 640 by 480 window appears, filled violet, and the terminal prints something
like:

@code{.txt}
Lumina 3.4.0 Win32 WGL Null EGL OSMesa VisualC
@endcode

_Escape_ or the close button ends it.


@section start_pin Pinning the version

`GIT_TAG master` means "whatever is on the branch when CMake happens to clone".
Two people cloning your project a week apart can get different libraries, and
so can your build server.  For anything you keep, pin to an exact revision:

Ask the repository what the branch points at right now:

@code{.sh}
git ls-remote https://github.com/Orvaen/Lumina.git master
@endcode

and write that revision into the file:

@code{.cmake}
FetchContent_Declare(
    lumina
    GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/Orvaen/Lumina.git
    GIT_TAG        0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567
)
@endcode

A full 40-character commit SHA is the only form that nothing can move under
you.  Once the project publishes release tags, a tag name reads better and
works the same way.  With either, drop `GIT_SHALLOW TRUE`: a shallow clone
cannot reach an arbitrary commit.

Updating is then a deliberate edit of one line, which is the point.


@section start_opengl About OpenGL

Lumina creates the context; it does not hand you the drawing functions.

`find_package(OpenGL REQUIRED)` and linking `OpenGL::GL` give you the functions
your system header declares.  On Windows that header is frozen at OpenGL 1.2,
so the program above stays within what every system provides: `glViewport`,
`glClearColor`, `glClear`.

For anything modern — shaders, buffers, anything from this century — you need
an extension loader, which looks the functions up at run time through
@ref lumGetProcAddress.  @ref context_glext_auto explains the options, and
@ref quick_guide walks through a program that uses one to draw a triangle.

Vulkan needs none of this.  Set `.context = LUM_OFF` in the descriptor so no
OpenGL context is created at all, and see @ref vulkan_guide.


@section start_alternatives Other ways to get Lumina

FetchContent is the shortest path, but it is not the only one.

@subsection start_submodule As a git submodule

Useful when you want the source in your own tree, visible and diffable, and
builds that never touch the network:

@code{.sh}
git submodule add https://github.com/Orvaen/Lumina.git extern/lumina
@endcode

Then replace the `FetchContent` block with one line:

@code{.cmake}
add_subdirectory(extern/lumina)
@endcode

Everything else in `CMakeLists.txt` stays as it is; the target is still
`lumina`.

@subsection start_installed From an installed copy

If Lumina is already built and installed on the machine — by you, following
@ref compile_guide, or by a package manager — ask for it instead of building
it:

@code{.cmake}
find_package(lumina REQUIRED)
@endcode

Again the target is `lumina`, so nothing else changes.  @ref build_guide covers
this route in full, including pkg-config and linking by hand.

@subsection start_subproject What Lumina builds as a subproject

Pulled in by any of these routes, Lumina builds only the library.  Its
examples, tests and documentation are configured only when it is the top-level
project, so they cost you nothing.  @ref compile_options lists the switches if
you want to change what it builds.


@section start_trouble When it does not work

- __CMake refuses the project with a message about compatibility below 3.5.__
  You are on a Lumina revision older than this one, with CMake 4. Move to a
  newer revision, or configure with `-DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5`.

- __`Could NOT find OpenGL` on Linux.__  Install the development packages
  listed in @ref compile_deps.  `xorg-dev` covers it on Debian and
  derivatives.

- __`Cannot open include file: Lumina/lumina.h`.__  Your target is not linked
  against `lumina`.  The include path travels with the target, so
  `target_link_libraries(hello PRIVATE lumina)` is what provides it.

- __Undefined references to `glClear`, `glViewport` and friends.__
  `OpenGL::GL` is missing from `target_link_libraries`.  Lumina does not link
  the OpenGL library on your behalf.

- __The window opens and closes immediately.__  Something set the close flag,
  or the loop was never entered.  Check the return value of
  @ref lumCreateWindowEx and read what the error callback printed.

- __Nothing is drawn; the window is black or transparent.__  Either no context
  is current — set `.makeCurrent = LUM_ON` — or @ref lumSwapBuffers is not
  called every frame.


@section start_next Where to go next

- @ref first_window takes the program above apart, line by line.
- @ref quick_guide is the longer tutorial: the same start, carried through
  to a rotating triangle.
- @ref build_guide covers every way of including and linking Lumina.
- @ref compile_guide builds the library on its own, with all its options.

*/
